@seelabs Because, as far as I know, inner objects are always and only created in transactor classes and related helper functions, what do you think about undoing the `STObject` ctor changes, and adding a `makeInnerObject` function that has a `Rules` parameter?
```
STObject makeInnerObject(SField const& name, Rules const& rules)
{
    STObject obj{name};
    if (rules.enabled(fixInnerObjTemplate))
    {
        SOTemplate const* elements =
            InnerObjectFormats::getInstance().findSOTemplateBySField(name);
        if (elements)
            set(*elements);
    }
}
```
Both `STObject` and `Rules` live in `src/ripple/protocol`, so there are no levelization implications.

If a new amendment is added that uses inner objects, the `if` could check for all of them.

Minor tweak:
```
        if (SOTemplate const* elements =
                InnerObjectFormats::getInstance().findSOTemplateBySField(name))
            obj.set(*elements);
```

Ok. I missed the `peekFieldObject()` change because I was focused on the `set`. I think this is fine, but another option to consider is that you could restore the `peekFieldObject()`, then set up the template here. The only real advantages I can think of offhand are that it would remove the need to create the temporary local, and it avoids a move assignment. The disadvantage is it adds yet another copy of that code block from the ctor. May not be worth it, but I offer for your consideration:
```
    // AMM creator gets the auction slot for free.
    auto& auctionSlot = ammSle->peekFieldObject(sfAuctionSlot);
    if (rules.enabled(fixInnerObjTemplate))
    {
        if (SOTemplate const* elements =
                InnerObjectFormats::getInstance().findSOTemplateBySField(sfAuctionSlot))
            auctionSlot.set(*elements);
    }
    auctionSlot.setAccountID(sfAccount, account);
[etc]
```
But enough bike-shedding.

How about something like this:
>        STObject auctionSlot = STObject::makeInnerObject(sfAuctionSlot, rules);
>        if (!rules.enabled(fixInnerObjTemplate) && ammSle->isFieldPresent(sfAuctionSlot))
>        {
>            auto& as = ammSle->peekFieldObject(sfAuctionSlot);
>            if (as.isFieldPresent(sfAuthAccounts))
>                auctionSlot.setFieldArray(sfAuthAccounts, as.getFieldArray(sfAuthAccounts));
>        }

This needs to be protected by an an amendment check. In general, when adding transaction breaking code, the code should follow the pattern:
```
if (amendment_on){
// do the new thing
}
else{
// do the old thing
}
```
It makes the code much easier to audit and makes it much less likely that transaction breaking changes will sneak in.

